#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125584} :activity/id #uuid "00abd2c0-69a8-5936-a61c-3bf473fe1def" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048515 :topic/id #uuid "a5c178bb-5c36-5147-b26e-1a8bcca7e43c" :topic/math-academy-id 1905} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125331 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125756 :tutorial/content "For any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{P(x)}\\right)  = 0.\n$$\n\nThis is because as $x$ increases, any polynomial $P(x)$ will grow without bound, and therefore its reciprocal shrinks to zero.\n\nFor example, the expression $x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is a non-constant polynomial, so we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^3 - x^2 + 1}\\right)  = 0.\n$$\n\nLikewise, the expression $(2x-5)^4$ can be multiplied out to become a non-constant polynomial, so we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{ (2x-5)^4 }\\right)  = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "53993a40-4ef1-5068-ac68-7d738ba2a963" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3125 :tutorial/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocals of Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "744f7f66-3061-568b-9ca1-83bca1d8c218" :step/math-academy-id 17038 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125495}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124573 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124386 :question/id #uuid "ca99dd0a-e2bc-5b6a-8138-ffaac453a0d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-773"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d13b888-0ef7-5194-a54d-435d9b344749" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050259}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565485}] :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocal Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f653ed64-212d-5ba6-9f81-2b68d9576679" :step/math-academy-id 2644 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125331}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125495 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124575 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124387 :question/id #uuid "96ecee6e-f551-5ecd-9991-0f962190f382" :question/math-academy-id "e-774"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b50ced1a-50ab-57f3-a60d-dd1d9d5bd997" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572200}] :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocals of Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "e035b05c-e5d1-5bb1-9218-53af6ba978ba" :step/math-academy-id 2645 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125585}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125719 :tutorial/content "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)?$ To find out, let's look at the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea736c37f855415e54d8b3a3775fab07159b5de4a368bee45bba13ae261372da.png)\n\nWe see that the curve approaches zero as $x$ approaches positive infinity. The same is true as $x$ approaches negative infinity. So, we write \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe same is true for $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ where $n$ is *any* positive integer. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so on. This is because as the size of $x$ increases, the size of $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ decreases." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc60ef45-0775-563f-b940-b75c2072bb10" :tutorial/math-academy-id 198 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "18483bb1-f2e9-5d0c-9086-c24211966036" :step/math-academy-id 366 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125493}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125720 :tutorial/content "Let's again look at the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/e8/e8b24529b70e32a32eb45fc9ce5d907ca74020a940b46fbf63b5386bf2d8cd9d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^-} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = -\\infty \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = \\infty.\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not the same, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nAny function of the form $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^n},$ where $n$ is an *odd* natural number, has the same shape as $y = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ and consequently the limit behavior is the same.  For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=-\\infty , & \\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})=-\\infty , & \\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})=\\infty , \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so on. For all of the above, the limit of the function does not exist at $x=0$ because the left and right limits are not the same." :tutorial/id #uuid "c947a280-46a8-5dc9-ba95-a6a2486bc4e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 200 :tutorial/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Odd Power"} :step/id #uuid "c7a8cf4a-7017-5fdf-b283-ad717892866d" :step/math-academy-id 369 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124626 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124286 :question/id #uuid "1fd5312f-c992-5218-bcfd-9bc235e9cd53" :question/math-academy-id "e-41"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f015a46e-7493-558d-a1d8-c2d90f9306ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051049}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572110}] :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Odd Power"} :step/id #uuid "b51ac528-c3e2-5c7f-b774-1a784b67608a" :step/math-academy-id 370 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125587}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125721 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$\n\n![](images/b7/b75253dcc54e0c833995611513b17cb8563249cb5aeb32078508d2c43556313b.png)\n\nWe see that as we approach $0$ from both sides, the function grows rapidly without bound. So \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^-}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right) = \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)= \\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)= \\infty.\n$$\n\nAny function of the form $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ (where $n$ is an *even* natural number) has the same shape as $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^n}\\right)= \\infty, \\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "298d4a07-7a45-5ab1-9d41-8d9609e000c2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 202 :tutorial/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"} :step/id #uuid "fca491c8-71f7-5bef-9cd1-85fad2457174" :step/math-academy-id 372 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124627 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124289 :question/id #uuid "08d460c2-32e7-5e20-86cf-4a1c2bf13c28" :question/math-academy-id "e-43"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "038fb60a-e596-5e48-b05e-f8414d454518" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565447}] :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"} :step/id #uuid "460493e8-2d85-52f9-b7dd-35205179f738" :step/math-academy-id 375}] :activity/title "Limits of Reciprocal Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124705}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572236} :activity/id #uuid "a655a425-a6ac-5f15-b67b-b68bfc4e40c2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048515 :topic/id #uuid "a5c178bb-5c36-5147-b26e-1a8bcca7e43c" :topic/math-academy-id 1905} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84d61063-c977-5de9-9ee9-751b5700caa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571767} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d21a9e8-24d8-5f1d-b050-6be0b52be222" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b3ded2ac-377c-5a79-a174-a4c453e1b17f" :question/math-academy-id "q-300982" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{7}{4{(x-2)}^{3}}\\,=$\n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{7}{4{(x-2)}^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x-2)}^{3}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${(x-2)}^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x-2)}^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot 0=0\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1086f6d0-8fdf-5383-ace5-80814d556034" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572237}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f2985fe-ea8d-5e52-b72c-0a10ddf54913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571683} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb23454-9977-5191-ad3a-c12baa1c41eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571706} {:answer/id #uuid "7405bf6b-6a0d-5b62-a398-8dece69260f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571737} {:answer/id #uuid "781f5cee-1530-50ff-9dc8-5cb5770aa8a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571745} {:answer/id #uuid "be83f313-b20b-5287-9aeb-a58682fcdc0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571880}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571737} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5abf857-ce1f-5315-be6e-f87c73fa5383" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "126b6d2e-f9ae-5f21-89ee-ad0b88bb4a14" :question/math-academy-id "q-46965" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x-3}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x-3}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $3$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{x-3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e3308ad7f61359df7fb4e1b0f32f1d373c703be34b353ffa100ae57c6c115e0b.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x-3}=\\infty ,\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5f34953f-75de-56f9-a16f-93dfa8832257" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd970ca4-531c-5efa-b2d5-8a2654d8cfb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571977}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571977} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d76b44d-6cd7-5750-b38b-774c366dedf2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "facb659a-1d6a-50ad-a009-4e0f9aa3e331" :question/math-academy-id "q-334183" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12}{9{(x+5)}^{4}}\\,=$\n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12}{9{(x+5)}^{4}}=\\frac{-12}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${(x+5)}^{4}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+5)^{4}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 0=0\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6127401a-4b3b-5612-a426-5ddbe9b9c8f9"}] :activity/title "Limits of Reciprocal Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571530}]}
